(   [Problem Statement]: Derive the Shannon-Hartley channel capacity theorem C = B * log2\(1 + SNR\), explain its physical interpretation, and discuss how modern communication systems approach this theoretical ceiling.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Information Theoretic Foundations:) Tj
T*
(   - Claude Shannon \(1948\) established the mathematical foundation of communication theory,) Tj
T*
(   defining the maximum information rate that can be transmitted over a continuous channel) Tj
T*
(   perturbed by Additive White Gaussian Noise \(AWGN\) with arbitrarily small probability of error.) Tj
T*
(   - The received signal Y is composed of transmitted signal X plus Gaussian noise N \(Y = X + N\).) Tj
T*
(   The channel capacity C equals the maximum mutual information I\(X; Y\) over all possible input) Tj
T*
(   probability distributions: C = max I\(X; Y\) = max [ H\(Y\) - H\(Y|X\) ] = max [ H\(Y\) - H\(N\) ].) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Formulation:) Tj
T*
(   - For Gaussian noise with power spectral density N0/2 over bandwidth B, the noise power is N =) Tj
T*
(   N0 * B. The differential entropy of Gaussian noise is H\(N\) = 0.5 * log2\(2 * pi * e * N\).) Tj
T*
(   - Differential entropy is maximized when the received signal Y is also Gaussian distributed) Tj
T*
(   with total power S + N \(where S is transmitted signal power\). Thus, H\(Y\)_max = 0.5 * log2\(2 *) Tj
T*
(   pi * e * \(S + N\)\).) Tj
T*
(   - Substituting these terms yields: C = B * [ 0.5 * log2\(2 * pi * e * \(S + N\)\) - 0.5 * log2\(2 *) Tj
T*
(   pi * e * N\) ] = B * log2\( \(S + N\) / N \) = B * log2\( 1 + S / N \) = B * log2\(1 + SNR\) bits per) Tj
T*
(   second.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Engineering Trade-offs & Approaching the Limit:) Tj
T*
(   - Bandwidth vs SNR Trade-off: High data rates can be achieved either by increasing channel) Tj
T*
(   bandwidth B \(e.g., 5G mmWave, Wi-Fi 7 channels\) or by increasing SNR \(requiring higher transmit) Tj
T*
(   power or closer proximity\).) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
