(   [Problem Statement]: Formulate the physical principles of Design for Testability \(DFT\) in integrated circuits, explaining full scan chain insertion, Automatic Test Pattern Generation \(ATPG\), and Built-In Self-Test \(BIST\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Testing Problem in Deep-Submicron Silicon:) Tj
T*
(   - Fabricated silicon wafers inevitably suffer from random physical defects \(silicon lattice) Tj
T*
(   dislocations, dust particles causing metal shorts, open via contacts\). While functional) Tj
T*
(   simulation verifies that the circuit design is correct, post-fabrication testing must verify) Tj
T*
(   that every physical transistor on every manufactured die operates without hardware flaws.) Tj
T*
(   - Sequential circuits containing thousands of internal flip-flops present astronomical state) Tj
T*
(   complexity, rendering testing from external chip primary inputs/outputs virtually impossible) Tj
T*
(   without DFT.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Scan Chain Insertion \(Scan-Based Design\):) Tj
T*
(   - Scan Flip-Flop Conversion: Every standard D flip-flop is replaced with a Scan Flip-Flop) Tj
T*
(   featuring an integrated 2-to-1 multiplexer controlled by a global Scan Enable \(SE\) signal.) Tj
T*
(   - Scan Mode vs Functional Mode: In functional mode \(SE = 0\), flip-flops operate normally. In) Tj
T*
(   scan shift mode \(SE = 1\), all flip-flops are chained serially into massive shift registers) Tj
T*
